// telugu-demo — Telugu script typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/telugu-demo.script
//
// Telugu is written left to right, so it needs none of the bidirectional
// reordering Hebrew and Arabic do. What it needs is work inside each syllable,
// and that work falls differently than it does for the other Indic scripts.
//
// Telugu builds downward. A consonant and its vowel sign commonly fuse into a
// single drawn glyph, and a consonant joined by the virama hangs beneath the
// base as a subscript. That inverts the base: where Devanagari's is the last
// consonant of a conjunct, Telugu's is the first, with everything after it
// underneath.
//
// Nothing is drawn to the left of its consonant, so no sign is ever lifted to
// the front of a syllable — no short-i as in Devanagari, no e or ai as in
// Bengali. What does move is the vowel sign of the base: typed after the whole
// conjunct, it is drawn on the base itself, so it is moved back across the
// subscripts to reach it. Nor is there a reph: a word-initial ra with a virama
// stays an ordinary letter rather than rising as a mark over the syllable.
//
// \font telugu selects the bundled face, in a regular and a bold cut. No \rtl is
// needed — the script is left to right.

\use{document}

\title{The Telugu Script in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Telugu paragraph}

Each syllable is shaped on its own and the syllables are set left to right. Within a
syllable the consonants stack downward and the vowel signs fuse with the base; between
syllables the line breaker finds its breaks at the interword spaces exactly as for Latin
text.

\medskip

{\font telugu 14 regular
తెలుగు ఒక సుందరమైన భాష. ఇది ఎడమ నుండి కుడికి వ్రాయబడుతుంది. ప్రతి అక్షరం ఒక హల్లు
మరియు దానితో కలిసిన అచ్చుతో ఏర్పడుతుంది, మరియు రెండు హల్లులు విరామంతో కలిసి
సంయుక్తాక్షరం అవుతాయి.
}

\section{Vowel signs fuse with the consonant}

A consonant and its vowel sign are usually drawn as one glyph rather than a letter and a
mark beside it. The same consonant carries each of the vowels below, and each combination
is a form of its own:

\medskip

{\font telugu 16 regular
క కా కి కీ కు కూ కృ కె కే కై కొ కో కౌ
}

\section{Conjuncts hang beneath the base}

Two consonants joined by the virama do not stand side by side: the second is drawn as a
subscript under the first, which remains the base of the syllable. A vowel sign on such a
syllable belongs to the base, so it is set beside the base and the subscripts hang below
the pair — in {\font telugu 12 regular ప్రేమ} the vowel joins the \italic{pa}, not the
subjoined \italic{ra}. Book, word, and friend:

\medskip

{\font telugu 16 regular
పుస్తకం   పదం   స్నేహితుడు
}

\section{No reph}

The consonant \italic{ra} takes no special form at the head of a syllable. In
{\font telugu 12 regular ర్క} the ra stays where it is and the following consonant subjoins beneath it,
where Devanagari would lift the ra above the syllable as a reph. After another consonant
the ra hangs below, as in these words for love and village:

\medskip

{\font telugu 16 regular
ర్క   ప్రేమ   గ్రామం
}

\section{Independent vowels and syllable signs}

A vowel that opens a syllable is written with its own letter, not a sign. The anusvara and
visarga follow the base as spacing marks:

\medskip

{\font telugu 16 regular
అ ఆ ఇ ఈ ఉ ఊ ఋ ఎ ఏ ఐ ఒ ఓ ఔ
}

\medskip

{\font telugu 16 regular
కం కః అం అః
}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font telugu 18 bold
నమస్కారం! ఇది తెలుగు లిపిలో వ్రాయబడింది.
}
